      </tr>
      <tr class="border-t border-[var(--color-border)]">
        <td class="px-3 py-2.5"><code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[var(--color-accent)]">ruby</code> / <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[var(--color-accent)]">wasm</code> / <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[var(--color-accent)]">python</code></td>
        <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-[var(--color-text-2)]">FFI shims — the gem native extension, <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[var(--color-accent)]">@kotoshu/wasm</code>, and the <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[var(--color-accent)]">kotoshu_native</code> wheel</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2 class="display text-2xl mt-12">The batch format — KOSH</h2>
  <p class="text-[15px] leading-7 text-[var(--color-text-2)] mt-4">
    For hosts embedding the engine, the C ABI speaks a single batch wire format defined in
    <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[var(--color-accent)]">ffi::shared</code>: every buffer starts with the
    4-byte magic <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[var(--color-accent)]">KOSH</code>, a version, and a kind
    tag, followed by length-prefixed records. One serialization serves every binding — measured 3–5× faster than
    object-by-object FFI — and it is the format the 2630 conformance vectors speak, so all language surfaces agree
    by construction.
  </p>

